WEBVTT

NOTE
Journey: Advocates — Week 18 Leader Video
Unit 5, Lesson 2: Resurrection

1
00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:07.055
So several years ago,

2
00:00:07.055 --> 00:00:10.910
I was talking to a group of students and I was explaining to them

3
00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:14.193
basically what's in 1st Corinthians 15.

4
00:00:14.193 --> 00:00:17.610
I was explaining the fact that without the resurrection

5
00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:20.539
Christianity would cease to exist.

6
00:00:20.539 --> 00:00:25.057
And I had once described to me that it's almost if the resurrection was a tiny

7
00:00:25.057 --> 00:00:25.810
little thread

8
00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:32.402
connected to the fate of the world and if we were to cut that thread everything

9
00:00:32.402 --> 00:00:33.450
would fall apart.

10
00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:39.050
I was explaining this to my students and the looks on their faces were priceless.

11
00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:43.450
You could tell that they didn't know anything that I was explaining to them.

12
00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:46.250
It was all new news to them. They knew the resurrection.

13
00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:47.750
They were good Christian kids.

14
00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:51.498
They knew that and believed that Jesus had resurrected and they knew that it had

15
00:00:51.498 --> 00:00:54.450
something to do or maybe everything to do with their salvation.

16
00:00:54.450 --> 00:01:01.050
But they didn't realize that what if Jesus hadn't resurrected?

17
00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:04.050
What if he had just stayed in the grave?

18
00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:08.250
We always talked about Jesus was crucified for our sins,

19
00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:10.350
but he was also resurrected.

20
00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:14.250
So there's that second piece in the equation that's really super important.

21
00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:16.072
Then I said to them,

22
00:01:16.072 --> 00:01:22.450
did you know that there's historical evidence behind the resurrection?

23
00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:26.550
Well, that was a whole nother look on their faces that I came and described.

24
00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:27.957
It was fascinating,

25
00:01:27.957 --> 00:01:31.967
but it was so encouraging to me because I knew that there was something to be taught

26
00:01:31.967 --> 00:01:35.929
here and there was something that these students were going to gain from what I was

27
00:01:35.929 --> 00:01:36.550
telling them.

28
00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:41.345
It's likely that you're going to get some of those same looks as you start to talk

29
00:01:41.345 --> 00:01:46.082
with some of your students about this because so many of students know that Jesus

30
00:01:46.082 --> 00:01:46.550
existed.

31
00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:51.550
I mean, let's face it. It's not a new thing that Jesus was a historical figure.

32
00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:56.550
You're going to get a few people on the fringe that believe otherwise,

33
00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:59.550
but all mainline history is Jesus existed.

34
00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:06.550
He was crucified by the Romans. He was buried in a tomb and what?

35
00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:12.550
It's reported that he rose three days after. Interesting.

36
00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:14.883
But there's history behind it,

37
00:02:14.883 --> 00:02:19.550
which is why we focus on Paul's comments in 1 Corinthians 15

38
00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:25.321
to help students start to see the importance of the resurrection before we dive into

39
00:02:25.321 --> 00:02:28.550
the historical reliability of the resurrection.

40
00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:32.890
We want them to really get the whole picture of it along with the fact that even in

41
00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:33.465
chapter 15,

42
00:02:33.465 --> 00:02:36.550
Paul kind of gives a little bit of that historical account.

43
00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:38.507
Like Jesus appeared to people.

44
00:02:38.507 --> 00:02:42.550
He appeared to the 12 and he appeared to more than 500 people.

45
00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:46.100
The way Paul says this is almost as if he's saying, hey, they're alive.

46
00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:47.550
Go check it out. Go ask them.

47
00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:50.550
If you don't believe me, it's no big deal.

48
00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:54.363
So as your students are going through their intro experience,

49
00:02:54.363 --> 00:02:59.613
we want them to start to think about historically documented and verified event that

50
00:02:59.613 --> 00:03:01.550
happened before their lifetime.

51
00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:03.376
So we want them to go into history.

52
00:03:03.376 --> 00:03:07.550
We want them to see in a good place to do this is really in their history books.

53
00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:10.550
How much of the Roman Empire do we know about?

54
00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:13.746
How much of Alexander the Great do we know about?

55
00:03:13.746 --> 00:03:16.550
How much of the Civil War do we know about?

56
00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:22.468
How much of the historical events that happened long before us do we actually have

57
00:03:22.468 --> 00:03:23.550
information on?

58
00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:28.550
We want them to start to see it because as much as we look at Scripture,

59
00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:33.550
because it is the Word of God and it's wholly inspired and it's awesome,

60
00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:35.944
we also could take a step back and say, well,

61
00:03:35.944 --> 00:03:38.550
what does this say from a historical perspective?

62
00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:41.872
And what does it say about Jesus as a historical person?

63
00:03:41.872 --> 00:03:45.550
What does it say about the resurrection as a historical event?

64
00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:52.148
So we want students to start thinking of the resurrection just for a moment as a

65
00:03:52.148 --> 00:03:53.550
historical event.

66
00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:58.818
So that's what this exercise is really designed to do to connect them to the idea

67
00:03:58.818 --> 00:04:01.550
that the resurrection is actually history.

68
00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:03.814
Okay, so for your storyteller,

69
00:04:03.814 --> 00:04:09.550
I would recommend going to a local college or a local Christian high school,

70
00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:13.692
finding somebody that's a history professor or a history buff or somebody that

71
00:04:13.692 --> 00:04:15.550
really just knows not just history,

72
00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:17.573
but how to tell history well.

73
00:04:17.573 --> 00:04:21.550
Find someone that knows how to tell the story of history.

74
00:04:21.550 --> 00:04:25.550
And I say that because that's really what the Gospels are doing.

75
00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:28.305
The Gospels are the account of Jesus.

76
00:04:28.305 --> 00:04:32.550
We know this, but it's a story that's describing history.

77
00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:36.194
So if you can get someone that can kind of do the same thing with another piece of

78
00:04:36.194 --> 00:04:36.550
history,

79
00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:41.414
you'll be able to help students start to make that connection and start to see the

80
00:04:41.414 --> 00:04:43.550
Gospels as this historical narrative

81
00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:49.088
that just sort of kind of wells up and culminates with the death and resurrection of

82
00:04:49.088 --> 00:04:49.550
Christ.

83
00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:53.550
It's a very cool picture. If you do it right, you can paint it really, really well.

84
00:04:53.550 --> 00:05:00.550
We want to make sure that it's not a huge issue among students, but it is out there,

85
00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:02.713
and they will run into this when they get to college,

86
00:05:02.713 --> 00:05:04.550
that the resurrection was just a myth, right?

87
00:05:04.550 --> 00:05:06.412
So, yeah, okay, Jesus died,

88
00:05:06.412 --> 00:05:10.550
and it's a myth that the church made up that he resurrected.

89
00:05:10.550 --> 00:05:15.050
And it's important that we kind of help students see that that's not historically

90
00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:15.550
accurate.

91
00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:19.550
And we'll get into more of why that is as we move forward the next couple of weeks,

92
00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:23.873
but it's important to kind of help students see that that is something that they're

93
00:05:23.873 --> 00:05:24.550
going to hear

94
00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:28.550
as they go into college and beyond.

95
00:05:28.550 --> 00:05:28.874
Okay,

96
00:05:28.874 --> 00:05:34.254
so students are going to be able to see at the end of this that the resurrection is

97
00:05:34.254 --> 00:05:35.550
actually historical.

98
00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:35.777
Now,

99
00:05:35.777 --> 00:05:40.550
does that mean that we can say that this is a for-sure thing and that it's absolute?

100
00:05:40.550 --> 00:05:43.550
Well, I would say, yeah, of course it is, but that's a bit different.

101
00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:46.550
But can we prove that Jesus resurrected?

102
00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:48.550
Well, not beyond a shadow of a doubt, of course not.

103
00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:50.550
But we can't do that with anything in history, right?

104
00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:54.781
We can't prove too many things in history beyond shadow of a doubt because we

105
00:05:54.781 --> 00:05:55.550
weren't there.

106
00:05:55.550 --> 00:06:01.550
But how history is done and what you'll see even the storyteller from the historian

107
00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:06.550
is that the one with the best evidence is the one who gets to say what happened.

108
00:06:06.550 --> 00:06:12.550
So the question is, is do we have the best possible evidence for the resurrection?

109
00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:16.550
So there's a couple of things that I want to make sure you can explore this deeper.

110
00:06:16.550 --> 00:06:18.550
You can get into a little bit more. You can present to your students.

111
00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:22.550
You can get into discussions about this within your small group time.

112
00:06:22.550 --> 00:06:24.660
If your students want to go a little bit deeper,

113
00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:26.550
there's some really cool things you can do.

114
00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:30.550
So the resurrection as history.

115
00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:35.550
Now, when we think of history, most people think of, well, it's the Bible, right?

116
00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:38.550
So the number one place that we all look is, well, the Bible.

117
00:06:38.550 --> 00:06:41.550
Well, the Bible could be viewed as a history book.

118
00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:42.550
That's the word of God.

119
00:06:42.550 --> 00:06:45.550
We don't want to make a textbook, but there's history in it.

120
00:06:45.550 --> 00:06:46.550
Okay, that's great.

121
00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:48.550
So there's some evidence there.

122
00:06:48.550 --> 00:06:50.550
So we have one piece of evidence in the Bible.

123
00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:52.550
But what do people say?

124
00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:55.550
Well, that's the Bible and you can't believe everything the Bible says.

125
00:06:55.550 --> 00:06:56.550
Well, we'll get into that later.

126
00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:58.550
Actually, we can, but we'll get into that later.

127
00:06:58.550 --> 00:07:00.550
But what else then, right?

128
00:07:00.550 --> 00:07:02.550
Are there other things?

129
00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:06.550
Well, what about outside of the Bible?

130
00:07:06.550 --> 00:07:14.550
Are there things outside of the Bible that give us clues that Jesus resurrected?

131
00:07:14.550 --> 00:07:16.550
There is, right?

132
00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:18.550
There are Jewish historians.

133
00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:30.570
There are Roman historians that recorded events about the resurrection,

134
00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:34.570
that at least the disciples of the early years thought that Jesus resurrected

135
00:07:34.570 --> 00:07:38.570
and recorded that they're reporting that Jesus resurrected.

136
00:07:38.570 --> 00:07:40.570
So that's important.

137
00:07:40.570 --> 00:07:53.440
There are early Christian writings like the Didache and letters from a church

138
00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:57.440
father named Clement that talk about the resurrection.

139
00:07:57.440 --> 00:07:59.440
These aren't in the Bible.

140
00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:03.440
They're good Christian writings, the stuff that we still kind of use and study

141
00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:07.440
in the world of scholarship to figure out what's going on historically.

142
00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:09.440
But that's awesome.

143
00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:10.440
Okay.

144
00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:17.440
And you've got even now skeptics, people that aren't Christians,

145
00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:28.360
are saying that Jesus was reported as resurrected.

146
00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:30.360
Now, this kind of raises the question.

147
00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:31.360
All right.

148
00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:36.360
Well, if all of these areas say that Jesus resurrected

149
00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:40.360
or at least give us clues to know that people believe that he did,

150
00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:42.360
that's pretty good.

151
00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:44.360
Well, maybe there's other explanations.

152
00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:46.360
Maybe there's other things that are going on.

153
00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:51.360
Well, every other explanation given has to provide more evidence,

154
00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:57.360
better evidence than the hypothesis, if you will, that he resurrected.

155
00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:00.360
And when you do that without going into all the details,

156
00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:03.360
when you do that, they all fall short.

157
00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:07.360
The one that says he just sort of fainted and that he did wasn't really dead.

158
00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:09.360
So therefore, you didn't have to worry about it.

159
00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:11.360
There's ones that he was buried.

160
00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:13.360
He wasn't actually buried in the tomb.

161
00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:19.003
He was buried in a shallow grave and some birds and wild dogs end up eating his

162
00:09:19.003 --> 00:09:19.360
body.

163
00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:23.360
There's some people that say, you know, they just grabbed a body

164
00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:26.360
and pretended it was Jesus and it really wasn't.

165
00:09:26.360 --> 00:09:28.360
And there's all kinds of different theories,

166
00:09:28.360 --> 00:09:34.360
none of which have the same amount of evidence as the resurrection.

167
00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:38.360
So this is exactly why we wanted to connect it with history.

168
00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:42.090
Because when we look at other historical events and how much evidence there are for

169
00:09:42.090 --> 00:09:42.360
those,

170
00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:46.392
we have just as much or more for the resurrection as pretty much most other

171
00:09:46.392 --> 00:09:47.360
historical events,

172
00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:51.360
especially historical events that happened around the same time.

173
00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:56.360
And we'll see that from when we talk about Scripture specifically.

174
00:09:56.360 --> 00:10:01.360
So help your students see that this was a historical event,

175
00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:03.360
that there's evidence as a historical event.

176
00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:06.360
But just like last week, I would do the same thing that you did last week.

177
00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:09.360
I'd go into your small groups and I would ask them,

178
00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:10.370
number one,

179
00:10:10.370 --> 00:10:17.166
are you convinced that it's at least possible the evidence points to Jesus

180
00:10:17.166 --> 00:10:18.360
resurrecting?

181
00:10:18.360 --> 00:10:22.360
And number two, if you are convinced, what does that mean?

182
00:10:22.360 --> 00:10:26.360
We talked in the very beginning about the importance of the resurrection, right?

183
00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:29.360
If the resurrection is out there, all of Christianity falls down.

184
00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:34.360
So if you're convinced in the resurrection, what does that now mean for your life?

185
00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:36.360
And what needs to change?

186
00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:38.360
What needs to be furthered?

187
00:10:38.360 --> 00:10:41.360
How does your spiritual walk need to go even further than maybe it is now?

188
00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:43.360
What does that mean for you?

189
00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:47.360
What does that mean for how you talk to other people about the resurrection in Jesus

190
00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:51.360
and who he is and what the crucifixion resurrection actually means

191
00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:53.360
and what salvation is all about?

192
00:10:53.360 --> 00:10:56.360
Ask students to sort of not just do they believe this stuff,

193
00:10:56.360 --> 00:10:59.360
but have them dig into, okay, now what?

194
00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:00.360
What does all this mean?

195
00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:02.360
What do I do with all this information?

196
00:11:02.360 --> 00:11:06.360
And if they're not convinced and they're still kind of going, I'm not sure.

197
00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:08.360
That's okay.

198
00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:12.360
But now is the time where you can start digging a little bit into,

199
00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:15.360
okay, well, what questions do you continue to have?

200
00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:18.360
What roadblocks are there that I can help knock down?

201
00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:22.360
What do I need to do to help bring you along a little bit,

202
00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:25.360
maybe better to help you understand these things a little bit deeper?

203
00:11:25.360 --> 00:11:27.360
Is it a misunderstanding?

204
00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:29.360
Is I'm just not getting something?

205
00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:32.360
Or is it something bigger?

206
00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:37.003
Now is the time that you might start to see some roadblocks and some students'

207
00:11:37.003 --> 00:11:37.360
lives,

208
00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:39.360
some walls they might put up.

209
00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:42.360
They might want to believe this stuff.

210
00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:45.360
They might really like love the idea,

211
00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:47.360
but they might still hold back.

212
00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:50.360
And as you walk alongside these students,

213
00:11:50.360 --> 00:11:52.360
you're going to need to start figuring out why that is

214
00:11:52.360 --> 00:11:55.360
and what's going on and start to, that's why we talked to the very beginning,

215
00:11:55.360 --> 00:11:57.360
the importance of building relationships

216
00:11:57.360 --> 00:11:59.360
because now it's going to start to really matter.

217
00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:02.360
If it hasn't mattered before, now it's really going to get there

218
00:12:02.360 --> 00:12:06.360
because there's going to be some major changes going on in your students' lives.

